  "body": {
    "id": "prop:uniform-wald-coverage",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "For every fixed \\(\\alpha\\in(0,1)\\), \\[\\sup_{P\\in\\mathcal P_4}\\left|P\\{\\theta\\in\\mathcal I_{n,\\alpha}\\}-(1-\\alpha)\\right|\\to0.\\]",
    "free_symbols": [
      "P",
      "\\mathcal P_4",
      "\\theta",
      "\\mathcal I_{n,\\alpha}",
      "n",
      "\\alpha"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:wald-interval",
      "thm:uniform-endpoint-efficiency"
    ],
    "justification": "The operational deliverable is an honest fixed-horizon confidence interval, not merely an oracle linear representation.",
    "gap": "Existing causal RMST Wald procedures require positive follow-up or high-level nuisance conditions and do not establish uniform coverage on the displayed quadratic-endpoint primitive class.",
    "consumer": "The result directly supports nominal confidence reporting for adjusted five-year RMST contrasts in CFsurvival-style analyses.",
    "proof_tex": "By def:wald-interval,\n\\[\n \\{\\theta\\in\\mathcal I_{n,\\alpha}\\}\n =\\left\\{\\left|\\frac{\\sqrt n(\\widehat\\theta-\\theta)}{\\sqrt{\\widehat V}}\\right|\n \\le z_{1-\\alpha/2}\\right\\}.\n\\]\nThe uniform Kolmogorov convergence in thm:uniform-endpoint-efficiency applies at both fixed continuity points \\(z_{1-\\alpha/2}\\) and \\(-z_{1-\\alpha/2}\\).  Since a standard normal distribution assigns no mass to either endpoint,\n\\[\n \\sup_{P\\in\\mathcal P_4}\\left|\n P\\{\\theta\\in\\mathcal I_{n,\\alpha}\\}\n -\\{\\Phi(z_{1-\\alpha/2})-\\Phi(-z_{1-\\alpha/2})\\}\\right|\\to0.\n\\]\nBy the definition of the quantile and symmetry of \\(\\Phi\\), the expression in braces equals \\(1-\\alpha\\), proving the proposition.",
